WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our first guest is
an Emmy-nominated actor,

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.238
who you can see in
the series "Modern Love,"

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:08.875
which is available starting
tomorrow on Amazon Prime Video.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.977
Please welcome Kit Harington!

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.378
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:16.382
-♪ It's Kit Harington,
this man's got the homes ♪

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:20.352
♪ As Jon on "Game of Thrones" ♪

00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:24.457
♪ But on his new show,
no dragons from above ♪

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:28.261
♪ He's a romantic,
that show is "Modern Love" ♪

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.329
-Dude, you got --
That's Kit Harington.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:32.999
[ Cheers and applause ]
Kit...

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:36.935
Kit, you've gotten two standing
ovations already tonight.

00:00:36.935 --> 00:00:39.305
-I did not deserve it.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.140
-I have to
thank you so much for doing

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.109
"Straight Up Goes For It,
Drops of Jupiter."

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:47.513
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yes!

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.448
What made you say yes to that?

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.917
-Yeah, I had a lot of thoughts

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:53.419
when you sent me that,
like, a lot.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:57.389
And the sensible part of my
brain, the bit that said,

00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:58.924
"You're not a singer,

00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:01.828
you've no connection
to this song,

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:03.897
it's of no relevance to anything

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:05.131
you're talking about
on the show..."

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.634
-That's correct.
-...said, "Don't do it."

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:10.669
But there was another little
part of my brain -- right? --

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:13.005
the bit that gets me in trouble

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:15.441
that was like,
"But what if you're brilliant?"

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:17.810
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.044
-Wow!
-Do you know what I mean?

00:01:19.044 --> 00:01:20.580
-Wow. You're right.
-What if you're brilliant?

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.049
What if it opens up
whole new vistas

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.483
of a career in singing?

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:25.752
-Wow. That's interesting.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.020
-Yeah.
-That could happen.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.889
-The thing is, that
10 seconds before I started,

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:32.124
that same little part of
my brain said,

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:33.526
"You shouldn't have done this."

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.862
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:36.862
-I just love it.

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:38.164
I just love that
you did it, man.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.899
You're just a good man.
I love you, man.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:41.601
I'll never forget that.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.637
We were talking backstage, and I
said "congratulations" to you,

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.972
because you just recently
became a dad.

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:48.041
-Yeah.
-That's amazing.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.041
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:50.343
♪♪
Yes.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.945
-That's a -- Congratulations.
-Thank you so much.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:56.116
Yeah, we're thrilled.
He's lovely.

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.784
-How old is he?
-He's 6 months old.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:58.818
-Oh, wow.

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:00.553
-So he's in that
gorgeous little stage.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.623
And, yeah, it all came about
because we went into

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.525
the pandemic, you know, me
and Rose, thinking --

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.861
not knowing how long it
was gonna last,

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:11.097
but getting all kind of
apocalyptic and romantic,

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.399
and, you know,
then we got a baby.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:15.568
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.336
And, you know, no spoilers --

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:18.570
I will never do that
to anybody --

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.406
but did you get any advice
from other parents?

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.740
-So much advice.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.876
Anyone who's a parent will know

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.310
you get so much advice
leading up to it,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.380
and it's all useless.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.548
It's all useless.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.616
It's all stuff like,
"It's gonna be great.

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:32.685
You're going to love it.
What a wonderful thing."

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:36.555
They don't -- No one tells you
that, essentially --

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:39.392
at first, anyway -- what it is,
is you get, like,

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:43.730
a new roommate
just dumped into your life,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.832
who you've not interviewed,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.833
who doesn't know
any of the rules,

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:48.834
any of your house rules.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:49.903
-Yeah, breaks all of them.

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.403
-Yeah, breaks
all your house rules.

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:55.875
And then you fall in love with
him, so you can't kick him out.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.310
That's basically parenting,
I think.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.014
-What's one of your favorite
parts of him?

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.347
-I just -- Do you know what?

00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:06.318
I go in in the morning,
and he has this bright smile

00:03:06.318 --> 00:03:08.554
and he's chuckling already
when I walk in the door.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:09.722
[ Audience "Awws" ]
Yeah, I know.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.391
-There you go. I mean, come on.
That's...

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:14.961
-But then he just dismantles
any plans I had for that day.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:16.529
-Oh, yeah, of course. Yeah.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.831
But do you make him laugh
and do anything?

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:19.998
You do anything to make
the kid laugh.

00:03:19.998 --> 00:03:21.066
-I do anything.

00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:23.336
Yeah, I think Rose is
the one who

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:29.041
he seems to respect and love,
and, like, she's his safe place.

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:32.478
And I'm just -- I just seem
to be sheer entertainment.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.579
Do you know what I mean?

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.147
And not even
very good entertainment,

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:36.716
just entertainment, you know?

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.152
-Yeah, but just like --
What do you do, like --

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.553
-Oh, I've --
You know, I do anything.

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:44.657
I dance for him.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.225
I think --
Are you a good dancer?

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.226
-Oh, I used to be a good --

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:48.861
Well, I thought I used
to be a --

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.596
I also thought
I was a good singer.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:51.830
-You are. No, you are.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:55.902
-No, I used to be a good dancer,
but you sort of --

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.637
He -- I put him
in his little chair,

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.939
and he likes dancing to
The Beatles.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:04.710
And so -- But he only likes it
if I do these exaggerated moves.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.246
So I have to kind of really --

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:09.849
That's -- I've worked out where
the dad dance comes from.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.485
-So, is that -- You do that?

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.854
-No, it's more --
it's more like...

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.889
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.563
-Dude, that's...
[ Laughs ]

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:27.266
Come on, bud.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.902
-That's -- Yeah, but now --
Like, now I realize --

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.705
I go back onto a dance floor,
and I'll dance like that.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.940
I've lost the
old moves entirely.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.074
-Oh, how do --

00:04:36.074 --> 00:04:37.109
-And then, in the future,

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:38.611
I know he's going to
mock me about it.

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:41.514
And I'll be like,
"This is your fault."

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:43.315
-Exactly.
"I did this is for you."

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:45.952
Well, you're here in New York
because you just --

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.187
You're filming a movie, right?
Right now. "Baby Ruby"?

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.723
-Yeah. I did a film upstate,

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.591
a beautiful little film
called "Baby Ruby,"

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:55.761
which I can't really
tell you anything about.

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:58.130
-That seems to be what
most of my interviews are now.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.431
I have people come on,

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:00.967
and they're not allowed to
tell me about anything.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.769
'Cause you're also in --
I think you can say this.

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:06.039
You're in the Marvel "Eternals."
-Yeah.

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:08.741
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah. Thank you.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.876
-That's out in November.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:12.278
-And you can't say
anything about that.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:13.713
-It's -- I mean -- Yeah.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.981
I seem to have
segued from a TV show

00:05:15.981 --> 00:05:17.216
where I can't tell you anything

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.918
on to a movie where
I can't tell you anything.

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.386
-That's great. That's exactly --

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.955
-Could tell you how that TV show
ended now, if you want, but --

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:26.959
-No. I loved -- You know, I was
giant fan of "Game of Thrones."

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.361
But when you do a Marvel thing,

00:05:29.361 --> 00:05:31.531
do they have lawyers
come into the room and be like,

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:33.933
"Hey, sign these papers, and
you don't even mess, like --"

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.434
-I just think it's an aura.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:38.103
I think that there's a kind of,
like, you just know.

00:05:38.103 --> 00:05:39.505
No one tells you.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.006
No one says
you can't say anything.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:42.875
Well, first of all,
they don't --

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.343
You know,
I didn't get sent a script.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:46.645
I just got sent,
"This is who you're playing."

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:47.913
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:49.548
And so I was like, "Okay."

00:05:49.548 --> 00:05:52.485
So I just had to Google
the character.

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:55.121
This is, you know, a hell of
a way to kind of work out,

00:05:55.121 --> 00:05:56.322
you know, who you're playing.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:58.023
And --
-What did you find from Google?

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:02.061
-Well, I found out that he --
I Googled him and I was like --

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.763
He's a character called
Dane Whitman,

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:07.933
and I realized he has
a sword and rides a horse.

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:11.404
And I was like,
"I know this guy.

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:13.973
I've been this guy."
-"I've been this guy." Yeah.

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:17.109
"I've crushed this character."
Yeah.

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.845
-But, actually, he's quite
different, so it's exciting.

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:20.279
Yeah.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:23.683
-And there was a rumor that
maybe Harry Styles is in this.

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:25.751
[ Crowd cheering ]

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:27.220
-I have no idea.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.722
Unless I've been recast,
and he's playing my part,

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.856
I have not a clue.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.491
-No. So you didn't
run into him or anything?

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:33.525
-No.
-No.

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:35.328
So you guys did
scenes separately?

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:38.964
-I -- Ooh!
[ Laughs ]

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:41.400
-Why would I try
to sneak something?

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.468
-Yeah.
-Yeah, I don't do that.

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:45.204
-No. No comment.
-Yeah.

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:46.839
But he's a
good guy though, right?

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:49.041
-Yeah, I hear
he's a great guy, yeah.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:50.476
Yeah.
-Yeah, you would know, right?

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:52.278
I mean, you spent time
with the guy.

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.680
-Yeah. lovely.
Lovely chap, I think.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.916
I don't know.
I've never met the guy.

00:06:57.916 --> 00:06:59.818
[ Laughs ]

00:06:59.818 --> 00:07:01.020
-See? You're a good actor,

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.055
so I don't know
what's happening right now.

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:07.859
Alright, I want to talk about
your series, "Modern Love."

00:07:07.859 --> 00:07:10.663
Your episode is up --
Well, it's available tomorrow.

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:12.965
What drew you to this series?

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.933
-I just --
I loved the first season,

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:18.671
and, quite honestly,

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:21.273
it was something lighter to do,
lighter to play.

00:07:21.273 --> 00:07:24.576
I'd spent nine years playing
a very serious character,

00:07:24.576 --> 00:07:27.380
who had zero comedy
to him at all,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.914
and I thought, you know,
this was --

00:07:28.914 --> 00:07:30.182
-Imagine if Jon Snow was

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:31.817
supposed to be funny
in that show.

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:33.118
That would ruin the whole
"Game of Thrones."

00:07:33.118 --> 00:07:34.887
-They tried every now and again.
They threw him a joke.

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:36.722
Just fell flat,
just did not work.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.791
Did not work with him.
They got cut.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:41.994
No, so, with "Modern Love," it's
just a beautiful little story

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:47.599
about two guys on --
Two people who meet on a train

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:49.735
on the way to Dublin.

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:54.340
And they sort of kind of
have this instant connection,

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.241
and then they
don't switch numbers.

00:07:56.241 --> 00:07:57.809
And it's at the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:07:57.809 --> 00:07:59.078
so they do it old school,

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.379
but they don't know
how long it's going to last.

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:02.214
And then, of course,
everything goes into lockdown,

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:04.250
and they've got no way
of finding each other.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.918
So it's whether
they find each other,

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:08.087
and it sort of
raises some questions

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:10.155
about what we all did
in the lockdown

00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:13.625
and where our moral lines lay
and legal lines and all that.

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:15.428
-It's shot in Ireland.

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:17.463
I'm assuming
that really was Ireland.

00:08:17.463 --> 00:08:18.764
-It was, yeah.
-Okay. Yeah.

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:23.168
John Carney directed it,
and it's where he's from.

00:08:23.168 --> 00:08:26.705
And, yeah, we were bunked up
in this little cottage,

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:30.242
quaranti-- We were quarantining
in this cottage,

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:34.413
and it was like being in some
sort of Amazon feedback loop.

00:08:34.413 --> 00:08:35.447
-Amazon feedback?

00:08:35.447 --> 00:08:37.883
-Yeah, well,
because, essentially,

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:39.385
we couldn't go anywhere,
so we were ordering

00:08:39.385 --> 00:08:42.154
all our food off Amazon
to be arrived,

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:43.989
being paid by Amazon,

00:08:43.989 --> 00:08:46.893
but the only thing they put on
the TV was Amazon shows.

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.194
So it was this kind of
circulating...

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:50.462
-Wow! That's a lot of Amazon.

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:51.764
-It's a terrifying glimpse
into the future.

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:53.765
-They're paying you,
but you're spending the money

00:08:53.765 --> 00:08:55.134
they're paying you on --

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:56.802
-Yeah. I felt like
I'd been duped into it.

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:58.770
-♪ The circle of life ♪

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.372
Yeah. That's so good.

00:09:00.372 --> 00:09:01.406
I want to show a clip.

00:09:01.406 --> 00:09:03.275
Here is Kit Harington in
"Modern Love."

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:04.676
Take a look at this.

00:09:04.676 --> 00:09:05.845
-Sorry. Uh, do you mind?

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:07.847
I'm just getting a crick
in my neck at this angle.

00:09:07.847 --> 00:09:09.815
Is it okay if I, uh --
-Yeah. Sorry.

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:10.849
Go ahead.

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:12.151
♪♪

00:09:15.787 --> 00:09:18.190
-Ahh! That's better.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.092
What were you saying?

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:24.430
-Can't remember. Sorry.

00:09:25.598 --> 00:09:27.833
Can I ask you a funny question?
-Sure.

00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:31.270
-When you first got on, how come
you sat over there with her,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.905
when you had the option of
the two tables?

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:35.241
-I just prefer to sit facing
the direction we're going in,

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:36.309
that's all.

00:09:36.309 --> 00:09:38.844
-Yeah. Now you're sitting
the opposite way.

00:09:38.844 --> 00:09:40.245
-Yeah, but I'm facing you.

00:09:40.245 --> 00:09:42.848
So my neurosis
can take a back seat for a bit.

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:44.316
It's not a big thing,
just a little quirk.

00:09:44.316 --> 00:09:47.253
It's not like it dominates
my life or anything.

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:48.821
-Do you want to swap seats?

00:09:48.821 --> 00:09:49.822
-Yes.

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:52.558
-[ Laughs ]
That's a good time.

00:09:52.558 --> 00:09:53.992
Kit Harington, everyone!

00:09:53.992 --> 00:09:56.862
Season 2 of "Modern Love"

00:09:56.862 --> 00:09:59.732
is available tomorrow on
Amazon Prime Video.

